// expect-error: wrong pool: handle was allocated from
// BUG-959 (refactor L stage 2): freeing on the WRONG ALLOCATOR inside a defer body.
//
// `alloc(Task)` uses the implicit per-struct auto-Slab; `heap` is a DIFFERENT,
// explicitly declared Slab. Freeing the one on the other is the mismatch zercheck
// already rejected everywhere else — and silently ACCEPTED here, because a defer
// body was raw AST that the CFG walk never saw. The wrong-pool rule simply did not
// reach inside a defer.
//
// This is not a depth or a scan-limit story: it was accepted with no nesting at
// all. Measured on the pre-fix compiler, which compiled this file clean.
//
// It is the accept-unsafe half of the reason the defer body is now lowered into
// the IR: one body, one set of rules, rather than a second statement emitter that
// implements some of them.

struct Task { u32 id; }
Slab(Task) heap;

u32 main() {
    *Task t = alloc(Task) orelse return;
    defer heap.free_ptr(t);
    return t.id;
}
